+getinfo({kind}, {name} [, {opts}])			*getinfo()*
+		Return a Dict with the definition of {name}.  {kind} tells
+		what {name} is looked up as: "function", "command", "option"
+		or "vimvar".  Another {kind} is an error.  The result is an
+		empty Dict when {name} is not found.
+
+		Every Dict has an "available" item, which is |FALSE| for a
+		function or an option that this Vim was built without and for
+		an Ex command that it does not implement.  The other items
+		depend on {kind}, and so do the items of the optional Dict
+		{opts}; an item of {opts} for another {kind} is an error.
+
+		"function"	a built-in function, or a user defined
+				function when there is no built-in one with
+				that name.  "s:Func" finds a script-local
+				function in that script, its "<SNR>12_Func"
+				name from anywhere.
+
+			For a built-in function:
+			name	the name of the function
+			kind	"builtin"
+			minargs	the number of arguments that are required
+			maxargs	the maximum number of arguments, -1 when there
+				is no maximum
+			method	the number of the argument that the base of a
+				|method| call fills in, zero when the function
+				cannot be used as a method
+			args	a List with a Dict for each argument:
+				name	the name of the argument as in the
+					help, missing when the help does not
+					name it
+				types	the types the argument accepts, a List
+					of |vim9-types|; "any" for an argument
+					that is not checked or is checked
+					against another argument
+				For a function without a maximum the last item
+				is for all the remaining arguments.
+			returns	the type of the returned value when the
+				arguments are of type "any", "void" when there
+				is none.  Also "any" when the type depends on
+				the number of arguments.
+
+			Example: >
+				echo getinfo('function', 'strlen')
+<			{'name': 'strlen', 'kind': 'builtin',
+			'available': v:true, 'minargs': 1, 'maxargs': 1,
+			'method': 1,
+			'args': [{'name': 'string',
+				  'types': ['string', 'number']}],
+			'returns': 'number'}
+
+			With the "argtypes" item of {opts}, a List of
+			|vim9-types| for the arguments, "returns" is the
+			type for arguments of those types, the same as what
+			a |:def| function is compiled with.  Required
+			arguments that are not in the List are of type
+			"any".  It is an error to give more types than the
+			maximum number of arguments or a type that is not
+			recognized.  The types are not checked against
+			"args".  Example: >
+				let opts = {'argtypes': ['list<number>']}
+				echo getinfo('function', 'sort', opts).returns
+<			list<number>
+
+			For a user defined function:
+			name	the name of the function, "<SNR>12_Func" for a
+				script-local one
+			kind	"function" for |:function|, "def" for |:def|
+			args	a List with a Dict for each argument:
+				name	the name of the argument
+				type	the type for "def", "any" for
+					"function"
+				default	the expression for the default value,
+					missing when there is none
+			varargs	only when the function accepts more arguments:
+				a Dict with "name", empty for "...", and
+				"type", "list<any>" for "function"
+			returns	the return type for "def", "any" for
+				"function"
+			abort	|TRUE| when the function aborts on an error,
+				see |:func-abort|
+			range	|TRUE| when the function takes a range, see
+				|:func-range|
+			dict	|TRUE| for a |Dictionary-function|
+			closure	|TRUE| for a closure, see |:func-closure|
+			sid	the script ID where the function was defined
+			lnum	the line number in that script
+
+			Example: >
+				def g:Twice(n: number): number
+				  return n * 2
+				enddef
+				echo getinfo('function', 'g:Twice')
+<			{'name': 'Twice', 'kind': 'def', 'available': v:true,
+			'args': [{'name': 'n', 'type': 'number'}],
+			'returns': 'number', 'abort': v:false,
+			'range': v:false, 'dict': v:false,
+			'closure': v:false, 'sid': 1, 'lnum': 1}
+
+		"command"	an Ex command, also with an abbreviation; a
+				user defined command is found first, as with
+				`exists()`.  The Dict is empty for an
+				ambiguous abbreviation.
+			name	the full name of the command
+			kind	"builtin", "modifier" for a command modifier
+				like `:silent`, or "user" for a user defined
+				command
+			nargs	the arguments as for |:command-nargs|: "0",
+				"1", "_", "*", "?" or "+"
+			range	|FALSE| when a range is not allowed, otherwise
+				the default: "." for the cursor line, "%" for
+				the whole buffer or a Number as with
+				|:command-range|
+			count	|FALSE| when a count is not allowed, otherwise
+				the default count
+			bang	|TRUE| when a "!" after the name is allowed
+			bar	|TRUE| when "|" ends the command, see |:bar|
+			register
+				|TRUE| when a register name is accepted
+			addr	what a range refers to, as for
+				|:command-addr|; a builtin command can also
+				have "none", "tabs_relative", "quickfix_valid"
+				or "unsigned"
+
+			Only for "user":
+			buffer	|TRUE| for a buffer-local command
+			complete
+				the completion as for |:command-complete|,
+				empty when there is none
+			definition
+				the replacement text
+			sid	the script ID where the command was defined
+			lnum	the line number in that script
+
+			With the "vim9" item of {opts}, |TRUE| finds the
+			command as in |Vim9| script, where an abbreviation is
+			not accepted and some commands do not exist, |FALSE|
+			as in legacy Vim script.  Without it the context of
+			the caller is used.
+
+			Example: >
+				echo getinfo('command', 's')
+<			{'name': 'substitute', 'kind': 'builtin',
+			'available': v:true, 'nargs': '*', 'range': '.',
+			'count': v:false, 'bang': v:false, 'bar': v:false,
+			'register': v:false, 'addr': 'lines'}
+
+		"option"	an option, also one that is not supported in
+				this Vim, see |hidden-options|
+			name	the full name of the option
+			shortname
+				the abbreviation, empty when there is none
+			type	"bool", "number" or "string"
+			scope	"global", "window", "buffer", "global-window"
+				or "global-buffer", see |global-local|
+			default	the default value
+
+			Example: >
+				echo getinfo('option', 'tw')
+<			{'name': 'textwidth', 'shortname': 'tw',
+			'available': v:true, 'type': 'number',
+			'scope': 'buffer', 'default': 0}
+
+		"vimvar"	a predefined Vim variable, see |v:var|.
+				{name} includes the "v:".
+			name	the name of the variable, with the "v:"
+			type	the type of the variable, see |vim9-types|
+			readonly
+				|TRUE| when the variable cannot be set
+			compat	|TRUE| when the variable can also be used
+				without the "v:", like "count" for v:count
+
+			Example: >
+				echo getinfo('vimvar', 'v:count')
+<			{'name': 'v:count', 'available': v:true,
+			'type': 'number', 'readonly': v:true,
+			'compat': v:true}
+
+		Can also be used as a |method|: >
+			GetName()->getinfo('function')
+<
+		Return type: dict<any>
+
+
